{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Ashurim and Letushim.</b> Names of heads of nations (Bereshit Rabbah). And the Targum of Onkelos — I have no way to settle it on the language of Scripture, for he explained לְמַשִּׁירְיָן, meaning a camp. And if you say this is not so because of the alef, which is not a root letter — behold, we have words that have no alef at their head yet an alef is added at their head, like ״חוֹמַת אֲנָךְ״ (Amos 7), which is from the root נְכֵה רַגְלַיִם, and like ״אָסוּךְ שָׁמֶן״ (II Kings 4), which is from ״וְרָחַצְתְּ וָסַכְתְּ״." "<b>And Letushim.</b> These are tent-dwellers who scatter here and there and journey, each man with the tents of his household. And so it says ״וְהִנָּם נְטֻשִׁים עַל פְּנֵי כָל הָאָרֶץ״ (I Samuel 30) — for lamed and nun interchange with each other."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:00:26.426619932Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}